1
00:00:00,362 --> 00:00:01,729
Here's a tree-planting crew.

2
00:00:02,212 --> 00:00:04,313
They plant fifty seedlings on their first day,

3
00:00:04,854 --> 00:00:06,815
eighty on the second and a hundred and

4
00:00:06,896 --> 00:00:07,776
ten on the third,

5
00:00:08,336 --> 00:00:10,418
thirty more each day as they get faster.

6
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:13,731
After twelve days, have they planted more or

7
00:00:13,812 --> 00:00:15,478
less than twelve hundred seedlings?

8
00:00:16,202 --> 00:00:18,550
Many people say less, or about the same.

9
00:00:19,134 --> 00:00:21,522
Twelve days at around a hundred seedlings sounds

10
00:00:21,563 --> 00:00:22,305
like twelve hundred.

11
00:00:22,928 --> 00:00:25,735
Let's find out, and see how arithmetic sequences

12
00:00:26,056 --> 00:00:27,098
model change like this.

13
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:29,848
First, why is this arithmetic?

14
00:00:30,591 --> 00:00:33,419
Each day's planting is thirty seedlings more than

15
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:34,001
the day before.

16
00:00:34,704 --> 00:00:36,588
When a fixed amount is added each period,

17
00:00:36,930 --> 00:00:38,894
the amounts make an arithmetic sequence.

18
00:00:39,678 --> 00:00:41,751
So a sub n is the number planted

19
00:00:41,893 --> 00:00:42,617
on day n.

20
00:00:43,261 --> 00:00:45,885
The first term, a, is fifty seedlings, the

21
00:00:45,906 --> 00:00:48,971
common difference, d, is thirty, and n counts

22
00:00:49,012 --> 00:00:49,332
the days.

23
00:00:50,175 --> 00:00:52,343
If the change were a fixed percentage instead,

24
00:00:52,685 --> 00:00:54,632
like three per cent more each day, the

25
00:00:54,673 --> 00:00:56,038
steps themselves would grow.

26
00:00:56,602 --> 00:00:58,778
That's a geometric sequence, and it's coming soon.

27
00:00:59,376 --> 00:01:00,743
Now choose the right formula.

28
00:01:01,469 --> 00:01:03,544
One day's planting is a single term.

29
00:01:04,248 --> 00:01:07,393
On day twelve, it's fifty plus eleven lots

30
00:01:07,434 --> 00:01:07,974
of thirty,

31
00:01:08,516 --> 00:01:10,319
which is three hundred and eighty seedlings.

32
00:01:11,081 --> 00:01:13,122
But the question asks for the total over

33
00:01:13,343 --> 00:01:14,302
all twelve days,

34
00:01:14,904 --> 00:01:16,284
and that's a partial sum,

35
00:01:16,865 --> 00:01:17,205
S twelve.

36
00:01:17,966 --> 00:01:18,566
It's six,

37
00:01:19,149 --> 00:01:21,873
times fifty plus three hundred and eighty, which

38
00:01:21,934 --> 00:01:24,618
is two thousand five hundred and eighty seedlings.

39
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:26,491
That's more than double the guess.

40
00:01:26,955 --> 00:01:28,724
The guess forgot that the later days are

41
00:01:28,866 --> 00:01:29,790
much bigger than a hundred.

42
00:01:30,355 --> 00:01:31,281
Look at the two graphs.

43
00:01:31,906 --> 00:01:33,872
Each day's planting goes up by the same

44
00:01:33,933 --> 00:01:36,883
thirty seedlings, so the daily amounts lie on

45
00:01:36,924 --> 00:01:37,706
a straight line.

46
00:01:38,409 --> 00:01:41,356
a sub n is thirty n plus twenty,

47
00:01:41,778 --> 00:01:43,381
a linear function of the day number.

48
00:01:43,963 --> 00:01:46,498
That's what linear growth means: the same change

49
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:47,625
in every period.

50
00:01:48,349 --> 00:01:50,777
But the running total adds a bigger amount

51
00:01:50,919 --> 00:01:53,427
each day, so its graph bends upward.

52
00:01:53,971 --> 00:01:56,541
The totals grow faster and faster, even though

53
00:01:56,643 --> 00:01:58,791
each day's planting only grows by thirty.

54
00:01:59,502 --> 00:02:00,307
Here's a bigger model.

55
00:02:01,011 --> 00:02:03,656
A new theme park has sixty-two thousand visitors

56
00:02:03,756 --> 00:02:06,201
in its first year, and three thousand five

57
00:02:06,241 --> 00:02:07,824
hundred more each year after that.

58
00:02:08,725 --> 00:02:11,910
Its visitors in year n are sixty-two thousand,

59
00:02:12,271 --> 00:02:15,476
plus n minus one increases, because the first

60
00:02:15,516 --> 00:02:16,617
year has no increase yet.

61
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:20,825
That simplifies to three thousand five hundred n,

62
00:02:21,227 --> 00:02:23,150
plus fifty-eight thousand five hundred.

63
00:02:24,094 --> 00:02:26,345
Over the first ten years, the total is

64
00:02:26,708 --> 00:02:27,170
S ten.

65
00:02:27,794 --> 00:02:31,001
That's five, times one hundred and twenty-four thousand,

66
00:02:31,343 --> 00:02:32,725
plus nine increases.

67
00:02:33,246 --> 00:02:35,690
So it's five times one hundred and fifty-five

68
00:02:35,731 --> 00:02:38,154
thousand five hundred, which is seven hundred and

69
00:02:38,215 --> 00:02:40,438
seventy-seven thousand five hundred visitors.

70
00:02:41,255 --> 00:02:43,360
In which year do its visitors first go

71
00:02:43,501 --> 00:02:44,664
over one hundred thousand?

72
00:02:45,449 --> 00:02:47,664
Set the formula above one hundred thousand.

73
00:02:48,168 --> 00:02:50,812
Then three thousand five hundred n is more

74
00:02:50,913 --> 00:02:53,697
than forty-one thousand five hundred, so n is

75
00:02:53,779 --> 00:02:55,922
more than about eleven point eight six.

76
00:02:56,847 --> 00:02:59,642
Years are whole numbers, so it's year twelve.

77
00:03:00,388 --> 00:03:01,120
Check both sides.

78
00:03:01,547 --> 00:03:04,819
Year eleven has ninety-seven thousand visitors, and year

79
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:07,188
twelve has one hundred thousand five hundred.

80
00:03:07,852 --> 00:03:11,207
Notice that year twelve comes after eleven increases,

81
00:03:11,690 --> 00:03:12,251
not twelve.

82
00:03:12,882 --> 00:03:14,677
Arithmetic models can go down, too.

83
00:03:15,462 --> 00:03:17,346
A new machine makes nine hundred parts in

84
00:03:17,406 --> 00:03:20,011
its first week, and fifteen fewer parts each

85
00:03:20,051 --> 00:03:20,713
week after that.

86
00:03:21,454 --> 00:03:23,238
Here d is minus fifteen,

87
00:03:23,779 --> 00:03:26,003
so its output in week n is nine

88
00:03:26,043 --> 00:03:28,047
hundred and fifteen, minus fifteen n.

89
00:03:29,110 --> 00:03:30,998
That reaches zero in week sixty-one.

90
00:03:31,882 --> 00:03:34,391
After that, the formula gives negative parts, which

91
00:03:34,673 --> 00:03:37,000
can't happen, so the model only works up

92
00:03:37,021 --> 00:03:37,321
to there.

93
00:03:38,205 --> 00:03:39,809
How many parts does it make before its

94
00:03:39,951 --> 00:03:40,953
output reaches zero?

95
00:03:41,656 --> 00:03:43,116
That's the first sixty weeks:

96
00:03:43,837 --> 00:03:46,578
thirty, times nine hundred plus fifteen, which is

97
00:03:46,739 --> 00:03:48,960
twenty-seven thousand, four hundred and fifty.

98
00:03:49,708 --> 00:03:51,555
Watch the wording about time.

99
00:03:52,459 --> 00:03:54,381
A young tree is one hundred and twenty

100
00:03:54,422 --> 00:03:56,965
centimetres tall when it's planted, and it grows

101
00:03:57,187 --> 00:03:59,089
forty-five centimetres each year.

102
00:03:59,772 --> 00:04:01,425
How tall is it after six years?

103
00:04:02,333 --> 00:04:05,063
Here the starting height is at time zero,

104
00:04:05,366 --> 00:04:06,750
before any year has passed.

105
00:04:07,556 --> 00:04:09,573
After one year, there's one year of growth.

106
00:04:10,258 --> 00:04:11,704
After six years, there are six.

107
00:04:12,308 --> 00:04:14,573
So the height is one hundred and twenty,

108
00:04:14,875 --> 00:04:17,722
plus six lots of forty-five, which is three

109
00:04:17,804 --> 00:04:19,307
hundred and ninety centimetres.

110
00:04:20,251 --> 00:04:22,174
As a sequence that starts at the planting

111
00:04:22,215 --> 00:04:24,740
height, that's the seventh term, not the sixth.

112
00:04:25,463 --> 00:04:27,088
So when the first value is in year

113
00:04:27,330 --> 00:04:30,140
one, year n has had n minus one

114
00:04:30,201 --> 00:04:30,602
changes.

115
00:04:31,365 --> 00:04:33,815
When the start is time zero, after n

116
00:04:33,877 --> 00:04:35,764
years means n changes.

117
00:04:36,515 --> 00:04:39,201
For any of these problems, first find a,

118
00:04:39,724 --> 00:04:41,026
d and n,

119
00:04:41,689 --> 00:04:42,751
and say what n counts.

120
00:04:43,518 --> 00:04:44,851
Then ask one question.

121
00:04:45,581 --> 00:04:46,492
Is it one period?

122
00:04:47,061 --> 00:04:47,931
Use a sub n.

123
00:04:48,561 --> 00:04:49,577
Is it a running total?

124
00:04:50,245 --> 00:04:51,191
Use S sub n.

125
00:04:53,704 --> 00:04:54,071
Your turn.

126
00:04:55,202 --> 00:04:57,585
A café sells eighty coffees on its first

127
00:04:57,625 --> 00:05:00,189
day, and six more coffees each day after

128
00:05:00,209 --> 00:05:00,369
that.

129
00:05:01,030 --> 00:05:02,713
How many coffees does it sell in total

130
00:05:03,034 --> 00:05:04,516
over its first fifteen days?

131
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:06,193
Pause here to answer.

132
00:05:17,068 --> 00:05:18,995
This is a total, so it's a partial

133
00:05:19,036 --> 00:05:19,196
sum.

134
00:05:19,819 --> 00:05:23,067
Here a is eighty, d is six and

135
00:05:23,349 --> 00:05:24,291
n is fifteen.

136
00:05:25,215 --> 00:05:28,487
Day fifteen sells eighty plus fourteen sixes, which

137
00:05:28,548 --> 00:05:30,053
is one hundred and sixty-four.

138
00:05:30,797 --> 00:05:33,181
So the total is fifteen over two, times

139
00:05:33,363 --> 00:05:35,707
eighty plus one hundred and sixty-four, which is

140
00:05:35,848 --> 00:05:37,812
one thousand eight hundred and thirty.

141
00:05:38,718 --> 00:05:40,330
One hundred and sixty-four is wrong.

142
00:05:40,714 --> 00:05:43,183
That's just day fifteen's sales, not the total.

143
00:05:45,082 --> 00:05:45,446
Last one.

144
00:05:46,453 --> 00:05:48,560
On the first day of winter, a woodpile

145
00:05:48,780 --> 00:05:50,526
holds one hundred and eighty logs.

146
00:05:51,208 --> 00:05:53,677
Each day after that, it holds four fewer

147
00:05:53,717 --> 00:05:55,021
logs than the day before.

148
00:05:56,063 --> 00:05:58,246
On which day does it first hold fewer

149
00:05:58,466 --> 00:05:59,447
than one hundred logs?

150
00:06:00,230 --> 00:06:00,996
Pause here to answer.

151
00:06:11,874 --> 00:06:15,015
Day n comes after n minus one drops,

152
00:06:15,537 --> 00:06:17,437
so the pile holds one hundred and eighty,

153
00:06:17,738 --> 00:06:19,759
minus four lots of n minus one.

154
00:06:20,724 --> 00:06:21,747
Set that below one hundred.

155
00:06:23,073 --> 00:06:25,438
Then n minus one is more than twenty,

156
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:27,643
so n is more than twenty-one.

157
00:06:28,526 --> 00:06:31,560
The first day below is day twenty-two, with

158
00:06:31,662 --> 00:06:32,505
ninety-six logs.

159
00:06:33,392 --> 00:06:34,487
Day twenty-one is wrong.

160
00:06:34,932 --> 00:06:36,802
On that day the pile holds exactly one

161
00:06:36,924 --> 00:06:38,331
hundred, not fewer.

162
00:06:40,055 --> 00:06:42,781
So an arithmetic sequence models a quantity that

163
00:06:42,843 --> 00:06:45,007
changes by the same amount each period.

164
00:06:45,329 --> 00:06:47,410
Its terms lie on a straight line, and

165
00:06:47,952 --> 00:06:49,872
S sub n gives the running total.

166
00:06:50,535 --> 00:06:53,192
But what if each step multiplies instead?

167
00:06:54,099 --> 00:06:55,061
Two, four,

168
00:06:55,625 --> 00:06:55,804
eight,

169
00:06:56,347 --> 00:06:56,728
sixteen.

170
00:06:57,392 --> 00:06:59,167
Each term is double the one before.

171
00:07:02,121 --> 00:07:03,005
Here are the key notes.

172
00:07:03,527 --> 00:07:05,296
Pause the video if you need more time

173
00:07:05,316 --> 00:07:05,798
to copy them.
